+# Delayed-header fixture investigation
+
+Baseline `2f07be5f66f64538a53e1593b16edac6242c8658`. This investigation is separate from the earlier slow-token oracle correction. Production seal `6ea343adb926647424dfffd8ecf77679b9d442b7e4391c2aff7c539580cc6525` and catalogue `b663097a02d007a6ff055946ae1c602fe9e16c33b3f9284e17a3b5ac187dc768` are unchanged.
+
+The unchanged delayed-header test passed five isolated fresh Windows processes, three original-prefix processes and five concurrent processes; Linux passed five isolated and three original-prefix processes. A natural failure was not reproduced in those runs. No surviving worker/server thread or socket leak was observed. This does not establish that the earlier reported failure was deterministic or Windows-exclusive.
+
+Read-only inspection recovered a synthetic OFFLINE_TEST failure record matching the prior publication time, 66-window catalogue and request=3/session=900 limits: WORKER_DEADLINE_EXCEEDED at RESERVATION, one spent reservation, zero completed batches, OUTCOME_UNKNOWN and worker_exited=true. The saved record does not contain a test name, so its association with the sole failed delayed-header test is contextual rather than a test-name binding. Its first window started at 564498.5114213, deadline 564501.5114213, attempted flag at 564498.8685288. No original TLS/dispatch trace survived; the exact OS scheduling stall cannot be recovered.
+
+The fixture's three-second deadline includes one second reserved for cleanup. Reservation, worker handoff, guarded file checks, TCP/TLS and send must all fit in the preceding two seconds before header waiting. The server counter increments only after complete HTTP request headers have actually been received and parsed; it is neither a completed-response counter nor proof of an attempted send. Conversely the durable journal's attempted/dispatched field is written conservatively before guarded HTTP dispatch; it is not proof OANDA or the synthetic server received a request. Zero received headers and a reservation-stage cutoff therefore do not demonstrate a transport dispatch defect.
+
+A clearly labelled, synthetic-only parent-clock cutoff injected immediately after the real durable claim reproduced the reported zero-request assertion failure and safe UNKNOWN/exited result. This is controlled evidence of the fixture's pre-header expiry path, not a claimed natural reproduction. No production deadline, token timing or authority defect was demonstrated. The fixture had no barrier proving that the delayed-header fault phase had begun before spending its short work budget.
+
+The six-path correction changes tests/fixtures only. The header-specific clock freezes setup at logical zero, then starts real elapsed time when the existing HTTPS server has received the actual approved synthetic request and checked its durable ACTIVE/ATTEMPTED reservation. A session/source-bound, immutable synthetic phase marker synchronizes the child worker. The server withholds response headers until teardown through an event, replacing its ten-second sleep assumption. TCP, TLS, request transmission, durable writes, counter observations, worker audit and fencing remain real. No requests, responses or counters are fabricated.
+
+The fixture is a header-wait diagnostic oracle, not a setup-throughput test. Its original request=3, session=900, startup=15, token=120 and cleanup allowance remain unchanged; post-barrier time is actual monotonic elapsed time. A separate 15-second real-wall watchdog bounds the complete fixture fail-closed and expires it if setup never reaches the barrier. All other actual-clock deadline, TLS, startup, crash and token-entry tests remain active. The existing fixed synthetic bootstrap admits only OFFLINE_TEST roots, the two exact immutable catalogues and exact fixture limit/fault profiles; LIVE and non-fixture packets refuse before credential entry. No production source, seal or CI job was changed by this correction.
+
+The original assertion of exactly one received request remains in failure(); the original accepted timeout reasons and HEADERS stage assertions remain AST-identical. An explicit phase-entered assertion was added. Existing no-retry, consumed authority, exited-worker and repeated-admission refusal assertions remain. Six additional tests cover real elapsed post-barrier time, setup wall expiry, scope restoration on exception, forbidden scenario/real archive binding, LIVE/non-fixture bootstrap refusal, malformed cross-session markers and actual one-request UNKNOWN/no-retry integration.
+
+Final Windows: all original 346 tests plus six added regressions passed (352 total). Linux: all original 134 supported tests plus six added regressions passed (140 total), using a SHA-256-verified source-only copy on native Linux temporary storage. On each platform, five isolated fresh-process targets and three original-prefix runs passed (eight target repetitions and 20 total test executions). Complete suites include actual TLS, crash, timeout, fencing and isolation regressions. Exact bounded real timestamps for durable reservation, worker startup packets, TLS completion, received headers, header wait and worker exit are in V28D66_HEADERS_TRACES.json. The worker result reports exited; no exact kernel exit instant is claimed.
+
+The 39 completed real batches, 126-file consumed evidence snapshot, 40 spent markers and 27,213-candle derivative passed read-only integrity verification and independent-process reopening. Window 40 remains consumed OUTCOME_UNKNOWN and excluded; the other 66 windows remain unattempted. Prior pilot and September archives remain intact. Scheduler remains enabled; unrelated scheduler modifications are excluded. No credentials, executable LIVE grant, authenticated request, new proposal, retry, trading or strategy work occurred.
+
+The refreshed package is 28 files and supersedes the previous 23-file approval. Nothing is staged, committed or pushed. Publication requires renewed approval. Production NO-GO.
