+# V2.8D66 timing investigation and fixture-only correction
+
+Baseline `2f07be5f66f64538a53e1593b16edac6242c8658`; previous reviewed manifest SHA-256 9a5cfff58ab2f6df9cc4c489a361075f6add739c37c8652a96bb1ad04b69cbb1.
+
+The failure is intermittent and sensitive to native filesystem/scheduling latency. Before correction, five isolated unchanged Windows runs produced three failures; five observed runs produced one failure. Native Linux source-copy runs initially passed. A short Windows source-only copy still failed under concurrent native load, disproving a source-path-only explanation. An attempted scoped-pinning optimisation also failed repeated load testing and was discarded. The final controller and source-seal bytes are identical to the prior reviewed implementation.
+
+No token-budget leakage was found. In the observed failing case, the simulated human token entry took about one real second, ended before the fresh phase was created, and the phase deadline was still exactly phase start plus two seconds. The fixture had overridden the aggregate session to two seconds; the existing one-second cleanup allowance left only one second for native reservation, repeated guard checks and HTTPS. NEED_WINDOW arrived 0.259 seconds after phase start; the claim completed at 0.972 seconds. A following bounded guard hit the cutoff before dispatch. The worker was fenced and the reservation remained spent/unknown. Passing observations reached TLS at 0.91–0.97 seconds. This was a host-speed assumption in a clock-rebasing oracle, not evidence that token entry consumed the new budget or that the server counter lost an HTTP request.
+
+The clock test now explicitly advances a fixture-only logical clock by one second instead of sleeping. Its token=4, aggregate=2, request=15 and startup=15 values are unchanged; all four original assertions are AST-identical, including exactly one actual loopback HTTPS request, an exited worker, at least one second elapsed before phase start and an exactly two-second fresh phase. The integer logical epoch avoids floating-point cancellation at host uptime exponent boundaries. The test is a deterministic rebasing oracle; it does not claim physical two-second native throughput.
+
+The closed test bootstrap accepts only OFFLINE_TEST, synthetic fixture roots, the fixed original catalogue, the existing crash-after-response fault and exact fixture limits. LIVE and non-fixture use refuse before any credential packet. It then runs the original worker audit, source/grant/role checks, real durable reservation, synthetic-token checks, loopback-only TLS and single-attempt transport. No request counter, response, durable write or authority check is mocked. Scope exit restores the real clock and original worker path. An additional real-wall expiry of 15 seconds expires the entire fixture fail-closed (the original fixture grant lifetime is 15+4+2=21 seconds). No configured deadline was increased. Real socket timeouts remain active; all other actual-clock token, deadline, crash, network and security suites remain unchanged.
+
+The six new tests cover exact clock advancement and wall expiry, clock/worker restoration, rejection of LIVE/non-fixture bootstrap packets, consumed token expiry without dispatch/retry, and an intentionally early phase. That deliberately early phase cannot pass the original freshness oracle and cannot dispatch.
+
+Production clock boundaries remain unchanged: startup begins at grant issue and is bounded by 15 seconds; the worker creates the absolute token-entry deadline before RESERVED (120 seconds in LIVE). After token validation and the credential role recheck, the parent durably creates the fresh aggregate acquisition phase (1,800 seconds in LIVE). Each subsequent window gets its own 15-second deadline, clipped to the aggregate deadline, before its durable marker/journal reservation; cleanup is included. The clock test alone substitutes the original fixed 4/2-second fixture limits. No HTTP is possible before the durable per-window reservation and attempted-state checks.
+
+Final verification: 340 existing Windows focused tests plus six new fixture tests passed, zero failures/errors/skips. Twenty plain and five observed Windows repetitions passed. Linux native temporary storage passed all 134 supported controller/diagnostic/fixture tests plus ten plain and five observed repetitions. Exact source-copy hashes are preserved in the external evidence. Early invalid observer runs, Windows-mounted WSL startup failures and discarded optimisation/fixture trials are excluded from final passing evidence. A Linux exploratory remainder failure whose ephemeral log was lost was not treated as a pass; the complete persisted rerun passed.
+
+V28D66_TIMING_TRACES.json contains bounded real monotonic observations, separate logical timestamps, state transitions and server counters. It contains no token, headers, bodies, account identifiers or arbitrary exception text. Synthetic crash-after-response cases intentionally end OUTCOME_UNKNOWN, with one actual fixture request and no retry.
+
+All 39 real completed batches, the 126-file consumed-session hash/size/mtime snapshot, all 40 spent markers, the 27,213-candle derivative and independent-process reopening were reverified. Window 40 remains OUTCOME_UNKNOWN, excluded from the immutable 66-window catalogue; its underlying LIVE failure cause is still unknown. No original authority or market-data record was modified. No real grant, credentials, broker connection, new proposal, runtime namespace, trading or strategy evaluation was created.
+
+No publication occurred. The correction changes only test/CI support; production seal `6ea343adb926647424dfffd8ecf77679b9d442b7e4391c2aff7c539580cc6525` remains unchanged. Catalogue `b663097a02d007a6ff055946ae1c602fe9e16c33b3f9284e17a3b5ac187dc768` remains unchanged. The complete refreshed 23-file package supersedes the previous 16-file publication scope and needs renewed approval. Production NO-GO.
